 {"q": "What is the significance of Romans 3:26, 'that he might himself be just, and the justifier'?", "options": ["God stops being just in order to forgive", "On the cross God neither pretends sin does not matter nor condemns the sinner: he remains just and puts the guilty right", "Only the just are justified", "Justice and justification are the same thing"], "answer": 1, "why": "The cross 'demonstrates his righteousness' after he had passed over sins, while he justifies those who trust in Jesus."},
 {"q": "Which verse is the best reply to the charge that penal substitution sets an angry Father against an innocent Son?", "options": ["Romans 3:23, 'all have sinned'", "2 Corinthians 5:19, 'God was in Christ reconciling the world to himself'", "Mark 10:42, on rulers who lord it over others", "Leviticus 16:8, on casting lots"], "answer": 1, "why": "The one reconciling is God himself, in Christ: not a third party."},
 {"q": "How does Calvin's view differ from Anselm's?", "options": ["Calvin says the ransom was paid to the devil", "For Anselm satisfaction is the alternative to punishment; for Calvin Christ makes satisfaction by bearing the punishment", "Calvin denies that Christ died", "Anselm says God was angry, Calvin that he was not"], "answer": 1, "why": "Anselm: 'satisfaction or punishment'. Calvin: Christ 'took the punishment upon himself'."},
 {"q": "Why does Calvin say it matters that Jesus was condemned by Pilate?", "options": ["Because Pilate was a Roman citizen", "Because a formal legal sentence shows Christ bearing the character of a condemned sinner, while the same judge declared him innocent", "Because it proves the Gospels are history", "Because it shows the Jews were not responsible"], "answer": 1, "why": "Calvin: a death in a riot would not satisfy; 'placed as a criminal at the bar', yet declared innocent, he suffers for others' crimes."},
 {"q": "According to Calvin, what is the relation between God's love and the cross?", "options": ["The cross made God begin to love us", "God first loves us, and so reconciles us to himself through the cross", "God never loved sinners", "Love has nothing to do with the cross"], "answer": 1, "why": "'It is because he first loves us, that he afterwards reconciles us to himself.'"},
